# Public edge for the remote connector (D-095, D-098; contract C4).
# Status: example. Mark each directive verified in docs/RESULTS.md when the connector probe passes.
#
# This is a second site, separate from the phone listener in deploy/Caddyfile. It is not a second
# Caddy process: only one process can bind :443, so add it to the main Caddy config with a top-level
# import (docs/MAC-SETUP.md, section 13).
#
# Why this site has no client certificate: the Claude client cannot present one. Access is
# controlled by the grant bearer token that the phone approves at pairing, by the strict path list
# below (anything else is 404), and by the gateway's per-grant limits. Do not add a client_auth block
# here, and do not add the phone's paths or the phone's port to this site.
#
# The public host name comes from CONNECTOR_HOST, set in the Caddy service environment (the launchd
# template sets it). Its DNS record must point at this Mac. Caddy obtains a public certificate for it
# automatically. Do not use `tls internal` here: a private certificate is refused by the Claude client.
#
# The gateway listens on 127.0.0.1:8789 (connector.port in the config). It is the only upstream.

{$CONNECTOR_HOST} {
	# The /mcp suffixed metadata path is forwarded for clients that look it up first. The gateway serves
	# only the root form, which the WWW-Authenticate challenge names (D-095, notes item 2).
	@connector path /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server /register /authorize /token /mcp

	handle @connector {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8789
	}

	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
